# Regression test for known-issues #6 (fixed): parse_module() built the dotted
# module path with `len += snprintf(buf + len, sizeof(buf) - len, ...)`.
# Because snprintf() returns the length it *would* have written, `len` could
# grow past the 256-byte buffer; `sizeof(buf) - len` then wrapped around
# (size_t) into a huge value and `buf + len` pointed outside the buffer, so the
# next segment was written out of bounds.
#
# That was reachable at *parse* time -- merely syntax-checking untrusted source
# (`--compile`, no execution) could corrupt memory.  An over-long module path
# must now be a plain syntax error (non-zero exit), never a buffer overflow.
#
# Only the non-zero exit is pinned, not the exact message text (same convention
# as step12_overflow.err / p52_recursion_limit.err).
